.\" libcgra.3 - manual page for the host CGRA library
.TH LIBCGRA 3 "2026" "libcgra 0.1" "Library Functions"
.SH NAME
libcgra \- host library for the UART-attached CGRA accelerator
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <cgra.h>
.PP
.B cc ... \-lcgra
.PP
.B "cgra_t *cgra_open(const char *" device ", unsigned " baud );
.B "void    cgra_close(cgra_t *" dev );
.B "int     cgra_identify(cgra_t *" dev ", cgra_info_t *" info );
.B "int     cgra_configure(cgra_t *" dev ", const uint32_t " cfg "[16]);"
.B "int     cgra_write_inputs(cgra_t *" dev ", const int16_t " west "[4], const int16_t " north "[4]);"
.B "int     cgra_run(cgra_t *" dev ", uint8_t " steps );
.B "int     cgra_read_regs(cgra_t *" dev ", int16_t " regs "[16]);"
.B "int     cgra_apply(cgra_t *" dev ", ... );"
.B "int     cgra_dot(cgra_t *" dev ", const int16_t *" a ", const int16_t *" b ", size_t " n ", int16_t *" res );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B libcgra
is the host side of a Coarse-Grained Reconfigurable Array: an R x C mesh (default 4x4, at most 16 PEs) of
16-bit signed processing elements (PEs) on an FPGA, driven over a serial link.
The usage model is always the same four moves:
.IP 1. 4
.B configure
\(em load one 32-bit word per PE that sets its ALU op and two operand sources;
.IP 2. 4
.B write inputs
\(em push operands into the west (per-row) and north (per-column) edge
registers;
.IP 3. 4
.B run
\(em clock the whole array a chosen number of steps;
.IP 4. 4
.B read back
\(em read all PE registers.
.PP
The higher-level entry points (\fBcgra_apply\fR, the \fBcgra_vec_*\fR kernels,
\fBcgra_dot\fR, \fBcgra_matvec\fR, \fBcgra_scan\fR and \fBcgra_conv\fR)
are compositions of those four. All kernels are in the installed library;
the CLI uses the same implementations for matrix, scan and convolution.
.SS The sim: emulator
Passing the device string
.B "sim:"
(or a supported variant below)
to
.B cgra_open
selects an in-process emulator that speaks the identical byte protocol and
re-implements the PE ALU, so the whole stack builds and runs with no FPGA. The
variant
.B "sim:flaky"
deterministically NACKs the first CFG and the first input-carrying transaction
to exercise the retry path, and
.B "sim:v2"
reports protocol version 2 and rejects
.BR EXEC ,
which exercises the pre-v3 fallback described under
.BR "FUSED EXECUTE" .
.PP
The geometry variant
.B sim:2x3
selects a rectangular mesh; append
.B :v2
or
.B :flaky
to combine it with a protocol or retry test. Positive dimensions with at most
16 PEs are supported. Unknown tokens and unsupported shapes fail to open.
.SH GEOMETRY
.B cgra_get_info(dev, info)
caches the ID reply and validates protocol v2/v3, 16-bit data and positive
dimensions with at most 16 PEs. The first call may perform an ID transaction.
All public kernels adapt to these dimensions automatically. On failure,
the identity output remains unchanged. cgra_identify always sends ID and
refreshes the cache, but only cgra_get_info validates support. There is no
hot-plug detection: after reprogramming or reconnection, restore the remote
parser, reopen and initialize again. Uncertain transfers invalidate the cache.
.PP
The legacy configure, write_inputs, read_regs, apply and exec functions require
4x4. They return CGRA_ERR_GEOMETRY for other shapes. For runtime dimensions use:
.nf
int cgra_configure_n(cgra_t *dev, const uint32_t *cfg, size_t ncfg);
int cgra_write_inputs_n(cgra_t *dev,
    const int16_t *west, size_t nwest, const int16_t *north, size_t nnorth);
int cgra_read_regs_n(cgra_t *dev, int16_t *regs, size_t capacity);
int cgra_exec_n(cgra_t *dev, uint8_t steps, unsigned flags, uint16_t mask,
    const int16_t *west, size_t nwest, const int16_t *north, size_t nnorth,
    int16_t *taps, size_t ntaps);
.fi
.PP
Counts are in words. CFG needs exactly rows*cols words; WR needs exactly rows
west words and cols north words. RD needs capacity at least rows*cols and
writes only that many words in row-major order (index r*cols+c).
EXEC accepts NULL inputs only with count zero, which injects zeros, and needs
exactly popcount(mask) tap slots. Absent-PE mask bits and unknown flags are
rejected. Length errors return CGRA_ERR_ARG before the transfer, although an
initial ID may occur. The EXEC wire mask remains 16 bits for all shapes.
.SH CONNECTION
.TP
.BI "cgra_t *cgra_open(const char *" device ", unsigned " baud )
Open a serial path (e.g.\&
.IR /dev/ttyUSB1 )
at
.I baud
(must match the bitstream, typically 115200), or the emulator
.RB ( "sim:" ,
.I baud
ignored). Returns a handle or
.BR NULL .
On failure errno is EINVAL for invalid device/baud, ENOMEM for allocation
failure, or the error from the failing POSIX operation.
.TP
.BI "void cgra_close(cgra_t *" dev )
Close the device and free the handle.
.TP
.BI "void cgra_set_timeout(cgra_t *" dev ", unsigned " ms )
Deadline for one complete reply, in milliseconds (default 2000).
Partial reads and interrupted waits do not restart the deadline.
Each retry has its own reply deadline; blocking writes are not bounded by it.
.TP
.BI "void cgra_set_retries(cgra_t *" dev ", unsigned " retries )
Retransmissions after a complete NACK for CFG, WR and EXEC with RESET
(default 3, 0 disables). RUN and RD are never retried.
This assumes intact command bytes and frame lengths. Timeout, I/O errors and
malformed replies stop the session without sending recovery bytes; later
protocol calls return CGRA_ERR_DESYNC. The failing call returns its original
error. See RECOVERY AND FAULT MODEL below.
.TP
.BI "int cgra_ping(cgra_t *" dev ", uint8_t *" version )
.TQ
.BI "int cgra_identify(cgra_t *" dev ", cgra_info_t *" info )
ID handshake; verifies the device signature.
.B cgra_identify
also returns the geometry (version, rows, cols, datapath width) in
.IR *info ,
so a host can adapt at run time.
.TP
.BI "int cgra_emulate_pty(void (*" on_ready ")(const char *, void *), void *" user )
Serve the emulator on a fresh pseudo-terminal (POSIX only); external serial
tools can then talk to a virtual 4x4 CGRA.
.I on_ready
is called once with the slave path before the blocking serve loop.
The path is borrowed during the callback; copy it if retaining it. The server
retries interrupted I/O, returns CGRA_ERR_IO on an unrecoverable read/write
error and CGRA_ERR_NOMEM on emulator allocation failure.
.SH PRIMITIVES
.TP
.BI "int cgra_configure(cgra_t *" dev ", const uint32_t " cfg "[16])"
Load a full configuration, one word per PE, row-major.
.TP
.BI "int cgra_write_inputs(cgra_t *" dev ", const int16_t " west "[4], const int16_t " north "[4])"
Write the edge inputs:
.I west[r]
feeds row
.I r
on column 0,
.I north[c]
feeds column
.I c
on row 0.
.TP
.BI "int cgra_run(cgra_t *" dev ", uint8_t " steps )
Clock the array for
.I steps
cycles (0..255).
.TP
.BI "int cgra_read_regs(cgra_t *" dev ", int16_t " regs "[16])"
Read all PE registers, row-major
.RI ( regs[r*4 + c] ).
.TP
.BI "int cgra_reset_datapath(cgra_t *" dev )
Clear all PE registers; the loaded configuration is kept.
.TP
.BI "int cgra_apply(cgra_t *" dev ", const uint32_t " cfg "[16], const int16_t " west "[4], const int16_t " north "[4], uint8_t " steps ", int16_t " regs "[16])"
One-shot configure + write + run + read back. Any pointer except
.I dev
may be NULL. cfg=NULL reuses the loaded configuration. Both edge pointers
NULL retain the current inputs; if only one is NULL, that edge receives zeros.
regs=NULL skips readback. RUN is always performed, even for zero steps.
.SH KERNELS
Element-wise kernels use the
.I diagonal
configuration:
.I a[]
enters from the north edge,
.I b[]
from the west, PASS chains route them to the diagonal PEs, and min(rows,cols)
results are produced per transaction.
.TP
.BI "int cgra_vec_binop(cgra_t *" dev ", enum cgra_op " op ", const int16_t *" a ", const int16_t *" b ", int16_t *" out ", size_t " n )
.TQ
.BI "int cgra_vec_binop_imm(cgra_t *" dev ", enum cgra_op " op ", const int16_t *" a ", int16_t " imm ", int16_t *" out ", size_t " n )
Generic binary map, with operand
.I b
either a vector or the immediate.
Supported operations are ADD, SUB, MUL, AND, OR, XOR, SHL, SHR, MAX and MIN.
Other opcodes return CGRA_ERR_ARG before device I/O. Stateful PE operations
are available through the low-level interface.
.TP
.B cgra_vec_add, cgra_vec_sub, cgra_vec_mul, cgra_vec_min, cgra_vec_max
.TQ
.B cgra_vec_relu, cgra_vec_addi, cgra_vec_muli
Named shortcuts: sum, difference, product, minimum, maximum,
.IR max(a,0) ,
.IR a+imm ,
.IR a*imm .
.TP
.BI "int cgra_dot(cgra_t *" dev ", const int16_t *" a ", const int16_t *" b ", size_t " n ", int16_t *" result )
Dot product accumulated on the MAC PE (16-bit wrap-around).
.TP
.B cgra_matvec(dev, A, rows, cols, x, y, y_capacity)
Matrix-vector product y=A*x. A is a row-major rows*cols array, x contains cols
elements, and y_capacity must be at least rows. Dimensions and capacity are
size_t. Row 0 computes products, later rows delay them; the host sums partials.
.TP
.B cgra_scan(dev, op, a, n, out, out_capacity)
Inclusive prefix fold out[i]=a[0] op ... op a[i]. op is CGRA_OP_ADD, MUL, MIN
or MAX. n and out_capacity are size_t; capacity must be at least n.
.TP
.B cgra_conv(dev, h, k, x, n, out, out_capacity)
Full convolution out[i]=sum_j h[j]*x[i-j] with zero extension. k and n are
the input lengths; nonempty output has n+k-1 elements. All lengths and capacity
are size_t. The implementation allocates a dense (n+k-1)*n Toeplitz matrix;
zero tiles are skipped during device execution, not during allocation.
.SS Kernel buffer and error contracts
All public kernels return CGRA_OK or a negative CGRA_ERR_*, never a length.
Capacities count int16_t elements. Array footprints must fit PTRDIFF_MAX bytes;
claimed lengths cannot prove the caller's actual allocation size.
Argument errors are rejected before device I/O or output writes.
.PP
Vector and scan output may equal an input exactly. Any other overlap of
output with an input is rejected with CGRA_ERR_ARG. Matvec and convolution
output must not overlap either input. Input arrays may overlap each other.
Dot may store its scalar inside an input because all input has been consumed.
.PP
An empty vector or scan (n=0) is a no-op and buffers may be NULL. Empty dot
stores zero through its required result pointer. Matvec with rows=0 is a
no-op; with rows>0 and cols=0, it writes rows zeros and A/x may be NULL.
If either convolution input is empty, output is empty and all pointers may
be NULL. Empty operations perform no I/O and do not check remote device state;
a non-NULL handle and valid opcode remain required.
.PP
Kernels replace device configuration, inputs and registers without restoring
the previous program. After a runtime failure, vector/scan output may have a
completed prefix; matvec/conv output may contain zeros or partial sums.
Dot stores its scalar only on success. Discard partial output on any error;
there is no rollback of completed device steps. Read-register and EXEC output
buffers are only published after validating the complete reply.
.SH FUSED EXECUTE
Protocol v3 adds one transaction that collapses the write\-inputs / run /
read\-back triple every kernel issues per chunk into a single round trip, and
reads back only the registers the caller taps.
.TP
.BI "int cgra_has_exec(cgra_t *" dev )
Non\-zero if the device implements supported protocol v3. The answer comes from
one
.B ID
handshake and is cached, so it is cheap to ask per chunk. Kernels use it to
fall back to
.BR cgra_write_inputs / cgra_run / cgra_read_regs
on an older bitstream. Zero also covers errors. To distinguish a v2 device
from a communication error, use cgra_get_info, check its return code, then
inspect info.version >= 3.
.TP
.BI "int cgra_exec(cgra_t *" dev ", uint8_t " steps ", unsigned " flags ", uint16_t " tap_mask ", const int16_t " west [] ", const int16_t " north [] ", int16_t *" taps ", size_t " ntaps )
Write the edge inputs, optionally clear the datapath, advance the array
.I steps
cycles, and read back the registers selected by
.IR tap_mask ,
all in one transaction. Bit
.I i
of the mask selects register
.I i
(row\-major);
.I taps
receives them in ascending PE index and
.I ntaps
must equal the mask's population count. Pass
.I tap_mask
= 0 and
.I taps
=
.B NULL
for a pure fused write+run.
.I west
and
.I north
may be
.B NULL
to inject zeros.
.IP
The only flag is
.BR CGRA_EXEC_RESET ,
which clears the PE registers before stepping. Besides saving a separate
.B RST
round trip, it allows retransmission after a complete, checksum-valid NACK.
Timeouts and malformed replies stop the session even with this flag set.
.IP
Returns
.B CGRA_ERR_PROTO
if the device predates v3.
.SH CONFIG WORD
One 32-bit word fully specifies a PE:
.PP
.RS
.nf
 31      26 25   23 22   20 19   16 15            0
+----------+-------+-------+-------+----------------+
| reserved | sel_b | sel_a |  op   |      imm       |
+----------+-------+-------+-------+----------------+
.fi
.RE
.PP
Assemble one with the macro
.BI "CGRA_CFG(" op ", " sel_a ", " sel_b ", " imm ).
.B op
is an
.B enum cgra_op
value
.RB ( CGRA_OP_NOP ,
.BR CGRA_OP_ADD ,
.BR CGRA_OP_MAC ,
\&... ,
.BR CGRA_OP_CONST );
the selects are
.B enum cgra_sel
.RB ( CGRA_SEL_N ,
.BR CGRA_SEL_S ,
.BR CGRA_SEL_E ,
.BR CGRA_SEL_W ,
.BR CGRA_SEL_CONST ,
.BR CGRA_SEL_SELF ,
.BR CGRA_SEL_ZERO ).
On the edges,
.B CGRA_SEL_N
on row 0 and
.B CGRA_SEL_W
on column 0 read the north/west input ports; the S and E edges read zero.
Arithmetic is 16-bit signed and wraps;
.B MUL
and
.B MAC
keep the low half of the product.
MIN/MAX use signed ordering; shift counts use the low four bits of operand B,
SHR sign-extends, and ABS(-32768) produces -32768.
.SH RETURN VALUES
Configuration and input words are latched before checksum validation.
A failed CFG or WR does not restore their previous values; resend a complete
valid payload before executing. A rejected EXEC does not reset or step the PE
registers, but its input words have been latched.
.PP
Operation entry points return
.B CGRA_OK
(0) on success or a negative code:
.B CGRA_ERR_IO
(\-1),
.B CGRA_ERR_TIMEOUT
(\-2),
.B CGRA_ERR_NACK
(\-3),
.B CGRA_ERR_PROTO
(\-4),
.B CGRA_ERR_ARG
(\-5),
.B CGRA_ERR_GEOMETRY
(\-6, unsupported shape/width or fixed-size API mismatch),
.B CGRA_ERR_DESYNC
(\-7, session stopped after an uncertain transfer),
.B CGRA_ERR_NOMEM
(\-8, workspace or emulator allocation failed).
.B cgra_has_exec
is the boolean exception described above.
.B cgra_strerror()
maps a code to a string.
.B cgra_open
returns
.B NULL
on failure and sets errno. Diagnose other functions through their return code,
not errno.
.SH OWNERSHIP
cgra_open returns an owned handle: close it exactly once and never use it
afterwards. cgra_close(NULL) is harmless. Device strings and input/output
buffers are borrowed for the duration of a synchronous call; no caller buffer
is retained or freed. The caller provides aligned arrays of sufficient size.
cgra_strerror returns immutable static storage that must not be modified or
freed. Setters/reset_stats with NULL do nothing; get_stats with either pointer
NULL leaves output unchanged.
.SH RECOVERY AND FAULT MODEL
Protocols v2/v3 have no delimiter, sequence number or protected command byte.
Checksums cannot reliably detect dropped or inserted bytes, opcode corruption,
or compensating payload changes. Automatic NACK retries assume that commands
and frame lengths were preserved. A command may already have changed device
state when an error is returned; there is no rollback or exactly-once guarantee.
.PP
After timeout, I/O failure or malformed reply, the library invalidates cached
identity and blocks further protocol commands on that handle. It sends no RST:
a controller still expecting the RUN argument could interpret RST as six steps.
Close the handle, restore the remote parser to idle out of band (device reset),
or allow its configured watchdog and all pending transmission/replies to finish,
then reopen and reinitialize configuration, inputs and datapath.
Reopening the port alone does not reset the controller. The emulator has no
watchdog clock. No fixed host sleep can guarantee recovery for arbitrary
bitstreams or broken links.
.SH INSTRUMENTATION
.B cgra_get_stats()
copies a
.B cgra_stats_t
(transactions, retries, tx/rx byte counts);
.B cgra_reset_stats()
zeroes them. Useful for measuring the serial round trips that dominate wall
time \(em and, since the counts are deterministic, for comparing a fused v3 run
against the same work on
.B "sim:v2"
without needing an FPGA or a stopwatch.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
.EX
#include <cgra.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    cgra_t *dev = cgra_open("sim:", 115200);
    if (!dev) return 1;

    int16_t a[4] = {1, 2, 3, 4}, b[4] = {10, 20, 30, 40}, out[4];
    if (cgra_vec_add(dev, a, b, out, 4) == CGRA_OK)
        for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) printf("%d ", out[i]);   /* 11 22 33 44 */
    putchar('\\n');

    cgra_close(dev);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.fi
.SH THREAD SAFETY
A
.B cgra_t
handle is not internally locked: serialise all calls, including statistics,
setters and close. Also serialise access to one physical device across handles
and processes; separate handles do not isolate the device's state. Independent
devices and in-process emulator handles may be used concurrently. The PTY
server uses ptsname's shared storage at startup: run such servers in separate
processes rather than alongside other ptsname users in one process.
.SH PROTOCOL SYNC
The protocol bytes and the config-word layout are mirrored in
.I hw/rtl/cgra_pkg.vhd
(device) and
.I lib/src/emu.c
(emulator). Keep the three in sync.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cgra (1),
.BR cgra (5)
.PP
